For your CrewAI Weather Multi-Agent project, I recommend implementing Human-in-the-Loop (HITL) as a separate application service between the weather orchestrator and the final response. Redis can store the pending approval and conversation state, while FastAPI exposes endpoints to approve, reject, or resume a request.

The key point is that your current weather application is primarily read-only. It usually doesn't need human approval to retrieve weather information. HITL becomes useful when the agent is uncertain about the user's location, needs clarification, or proposes a consequential action such as sending a weather alert or notification.

## 1. Recommended architecture

FastAPI — `/weather/agent`

Validate query, authenticate user, resolve session

Weather Agent Service

Check context, intent, and guardrails

HITL Decision Service

Does this request need clarification or approval?

No

Run weather orchestrator

Yes

Save pending request in Redis

Human interaction

Clarify location, approve, or reject

Resume processing

Execute permitted work and update conversation context

Your existing CrewAI orchestrator, weather tools, forecast tool, FAQ tool, and Redis conversation history can remain in place. You are adding a workflow control layer rather than replacing your multi-agent architecture.

## 2. Define the Human-in-the-Loop workflow

For your project, implement three HITL scenarios.

| Scenario               | Example                                          | System behavior                                                  |
| ---------------------- | ------------------------------------------------ | ---------------------------------------------------------------- |
| Location clarification | “Will it rain tomorrow?” with no known city      | Pause and ask for the city                                       |
| Context confirmation   | “I moved to Delhi” but stored context says Noida | Update the user's preferred location after resolving the request |
| Action approval        | “Send a severe-weather alert to all users”       | Pause until an authorized person approves                        |

Important distinction: Asking a user which city they mean is clarification. Approving an alert that will be sent to many users is human authorization. Implement them as separate workflow states.

